#
# The ARMADA's offload gate, written again once the system is up.
#
# The early hook (07-npuep) loads the driver and writes the gate as soon as the front ports exist,
# but it allows the coprocessor's rpc channel twenty seconds to open and runs before syslogd, so
# when the channel is late the write is refused and the refusal is lost (issue 406): the appliance
# then runs with the setting on and the hardware off. This hook runs at the "start" stage, after
# syslogd and after cleanvar has emptied /var/run, leaves a token for this boot there, and writes
# the gate again - waiting longer for the channel, and logging what the coprocessor said either
# way. offload.sh stamps its state with the token, and status.py calls the state this boot's only
# while the two agree: a clock-proof stamp, where kern.boottime moves when the clock is stepped
# and this kernel has no kern.boot_id. On a machine without the ARMADA driver it exits at once.
: ${BOOT_TOKEN:=/var/run/xgs-npuep-boot}
: ${OFFLOAD:=/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/xgs/offload.sh}
: ${WAIT:=90}
log() { /usr/bin/logger -t npuep -p daemon.notice "$1"; }

[ -n "$(/sbin/sysctl -n dev.npuep.0.rpc_channel.state 2>/dev/null)" ] || exit 0
[ -x "${OFFLOAD}" ] || exit 0

if [ ! -s "${BOOT_TOKEN}" ]; then
    /usr/bin/od -An -N8 -tx8 /dev/urandom 2>/dev/null | /usr/bin/tr -d ' \n' > "${BOOT_TOKEN}"
    echo >> "${BOOT_TOKEN}"
fi

i=0
while [ ${i} -lt "${WAIT}" ]; do
    case "$(/sbin/sysctl -n dev.npuep.0.rpc_channel.state 2>/dev/null | head -1)" in
    open*) break ;;
    esac
    i=$((i + 1))
    sleep 1
done
log "offload gate at start, channel waited ${i}s: $("${OFFLOAD}" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\n')"
exit 0
